    {
      "id": "Ks0WbmypBQS3GEflXC6wA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2060,
      "y": 282.5,
      "width": 1087.22998046875,
      "height": 493.63385416666625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2m",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 389667570,
      "version": 142,
      "versionNonce": 59432494,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782248392484,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**** Core component\n1. Frontier Queue: The queue of URLs we need to crawl. \nWe will start with a set of seed URLs and add new URLs as we crawl the web. \nThe technology used could be something like Kafka, Redis, or SQS. We'll decide on the technology later.\n--\n2. Crawler: Fetches web pages, extracts text data, and extracts new URLs to add to the frontier queue. \nHow to scale this component to handle the 10B pages we need to crawl???\n---\n3. DNS: Resolves domain names to IP addresses so that the crawler can fetch the web pages. \nThere are interesting discussions to be had about how to cache DNS lookups, handle DNS failures, \nand ensure that we are not overloading DNS servers. Again, more on this later.\n---\n4. Webpage: The external server that hosts the web pages we are crawling. \nWe'll fetch the HTML from these servers and extract the text data.\n--- \n5. S3 Text Data: This is where we'll store the text data we extract from the web pages. \nWe choose S3 as our blob storage because it is highly scalable and durable. \nIt is designed to store large amounts of data cheaply. \nOther hosted storage solutions like Google Cloud Storage or Azure Blob Storage could also be used.",
      "fontSize": 20.784583333333316,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**** Core component\n1. Frontier Queue: The queue of URLs we need to crawl. \nWe will start with a set of seed URLs and add new URLs as we crawl the web. \nThe technology used could be something like Kafka, Redis, or SQS. We'll decide on the technology later.\n--\n2. Crawler: Fetches web pages, extracts text data, and extracts new URLs to add to the frontier queue. \nHow to scale this component to handle the 10B pages we need to crawl???\n---\n3. DNS: Resolves domain names to IP addresses so that the crawler can fetch the web pages. \nThere are interesting discussions to be had about how to cache DNS lookups, handle DNS failures, \nand ensure that we are not overloading DNS servers. Again, more on this later.\n---\n4. Webpage: The external server that hosts the web pages we are crawling. \nWe'll fetch the HTML from these servers and extract the text data.\n--- \n5. S3 Text Data: This is where we'll store the text data we extract from the web pages. \nWe choose S3 as our blob storage because it is highly scalable and durable. \nIt is designed to store large amounts of data cheaply. \nOther hosted storage solutions like Google Cloud Storage or Azure Blob Storage could also be used.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
