    {
      "id": "J1VzZdGEAfc0dPL4GyIx0",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 992,
      "y": 936.5,
      "width": 1420.6590576171875,
      "height": 1275,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b3J",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 59087538,
      "version": 2583,
      "versionNonce": 1133108402,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1782257289042,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "**** How can we make sure system executes jobs within 2s of their schedule times?\n1. Query the DB: query Execution table for jobs that are due for execution in the next ~5mins\n2. Message queue: take list of jobs returned by our query and push them to message queue,\nordered by execution_time. Workers pull jobs from queue in order and execute jobs\n(using RabitMQ or SQS)\n--> pick SQS with native support for delay message delivery. When scheduling a job, \nwe simply send a message to SQS with a delay value (called a \"SQS Delivery Delay\"), \nand SQS handles the rest.\n\n**** How can we ensure system is scalable to support up to 10k jobs/sec?\n-- To handle high job creation rates, we can introduce a message queue like Kafka or RabbitMQ \nbetween our API and Job Creation service. This queue acts as a buffer during traffic spikes, \nallowing the Job Creation service to process requests at a sustainable rate rather than being overwhelmed.\n-- enable horizontal scaling, adding more Job creation service instance as consumer,\nensure no job creation request are lost\n\n*** Job DB\n-- Job table -- partition by job_id, which distributes write evenly across partition\n-- Execution table -- partition by time_bucket. \n\n*** Message Queue Capacity\n-- SQS: for 5min window, we process ~3million jobs (10k jobs/second * 300 seconds)\n-- with standard queue, throughput is virtually unlimited, so our 10000 message/sec\nrequirement is within capability. We can still want multiple queues for functional seperation,\nnot for scaling purposes\n\n*** Worker (AWS)\n1. Container (ECS, Kubernetes): more cost effective for steady workload, better suited\nfor long running jobs, since they maintain state between executions. Require more operational\noverhead.\n2. Lambda function (serverless option) -- short lived job under 15mins, can autoscale instantly\nto match workload. the Cons is that cold start could impact 2-sec precision requirement\n==> Given requirement of 2-sec precision, we will use ECS and auto-scaling group policy\n\n*** How can we ensure at-least-once execution of jobs?\nwe want to ensure job is retried when failed before giving up.\n-- SQS provide bulit-in mechanism for handling worker failure through visibilty timeout\n-- When a worker receives a message from the queue, SQS automatically makes that message invisible to other workers for a configurable period. \nThe worker processes the message and deletes it upon successful completion. \nIf the worker crashes or fails to process the message within the visibility timeout period, \nSQS automatically makes the message visible again for other workers to process.\n-- Periodically \"heartbeat\" check by calling \"ChangeMessageVisibilityAPI\" to extend the timeout\n\n-- instead of dedup job design, making sure we have idempotent job design, \n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "**** How can we make sure system executes jobs within 2s of their schedule times?\n1. Query the DB: query Execution table for jobs that are due for execution in the next ~5mins\n2. Message queue: take list of jobs returned by our query and push them to message queue,\nordered by execution_time. Workers pull jobs from queue in order and execute jobs\n(using RabitMQ or SQS)\n--> pick SQS with native support for delay message delivery. When scheduling a job, \nwe simply send a message to SQS with a delay value (called a \"SQS Delivery Delay\"), \nand SQS handles the rest.\n\n**** How can we ensure system is scalable to support up to 10k jobs/sec?\n-- To handle high job creation rates, we can introduce a message queue like Kafka or RabbitMQ \nbetween our API and Job Creation service. This queue acts as a buffer during traffic spikes, \nallowing the Job Creation service to process requests at a sustainable rate rather than being overwhelmed.\n-- enable horizontal scaling, adding more Job creation service instance as consumer,\nensure no job creation request are lost\n\n*** Job DB\n-- Job table -- partition by job_id, which distributes write evenly across partition\n-- Execution table -- partition by time_bucket. \n\n*** Message Queue Capacity\n-- SQS: for 5min window, we process ~3million jobs (10k jobs/second * 300 seconds)\n-- with standard queue, throughput is virtually unlimited, so our 10000 message/sec\nrequirement is within capability. We can still want multiple queues for functional seperation,\nnot for scaling purposes\n\n*** Worker (AWS)\n1. Container (ECS, Kubernetes): more cost effective for steady workload, better suited\nfor long running jobs, since they maintain state between executions. Require more operational\noverhead.\n2. Lambda function (serverless option) -- short lived job under 15mins, can autoscale instantly\nto match workload. the Cons is that cold start could impact 2-sec precision requirement\n==> Given requirement of 2-sec precision, we will use ECS and auto-scaling group policy\n\n*** How can we ensure at-least-once execution of jobs?\nwe want to ensure job is retried when failed before giving up.\n-- SQS provide bulit-in mechanism for handling worker failure through visibilty timeout\n-- When a worker receives a message from the queue, SQS automatically makes that message invisible to other workers for a configurable period. \nThe worker processes the message and deletes it upon successful completion. \nIf the worker crashes or fails to process the message within the visibility timeout period, \nSQS automatically makes the message visible again for other workers to process.\n-- Periodically \"heartbeat\" check by calling \"ChangeMessageVisibilityAPI\" to extend the timeout\n\n-- instead of dedup job design, making sure we have idempotent job design, \n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
